(148 "2026-09-27T04:26:01Z" "xl1" claim "T44" "xl1")
(149 "2026-09-27T04:27:54Z" "xl1" note "T44" "steer/lang.rkt: a gate registry (name exts unit check); lang-check returns (fs n lang unit); unknown extension -> one info 'skipped' finding, counted as neither clean nor broken. Both cmd-syntax and post-edit-problems route through it. The hook stays silent on unsupported files on purpose (a note per .md edit would be ignored); steer syntax reports 'skipped'. Before: a valid .py got a false read-error from the Racket reader; now skipped until T45 adds the python gate.")
(150 "2026-09-27T04:27:56Z" "xl1" refresh "T44" "2 anchors")
(151 "2026-09-27T04:28:19Z" "xl1" done "T44" "1 check passed")
(152 "2026-09-27T04:28:20Z" "xl1" claim "T45" "xl1")
(153 "2026-09-27T04:36:24Z" "xl1" note "T45" "python.rkt: worker script embedded, run as python3 -I -S -c (isolated, no site). compile(ast) not just parse, so semantic errors (return outside function) are found. Edits are candidate-and-verify: a small family of edits near the error (closer before a header's colon, at a statement's end, before a mismatched closer, delete an unmatched one, add a colon), earliest first, 2 s budget, offered only if the file compiles afterwards. First version tried only the statement end: 9/41 deletions got an edit; after adding header/colon and mismatch candidates: 36/41 get one and 34/41 restore the original exactly (misses: 3 braces inside an f-string, a closer at the start of a signature line). Timing could not be verified: load average was 334; bare python startup was 62-102 ms, gate adds ~40 ms.")
(154 "2026-09-27T04:36:57Z" "xl1" done "T45" "1 check passed")
(155 "2026-09-27T04:36:57Z" "xl1" claim "T46" "xl1")
(156 "2026-09-27T04:43:34Z" "xl1" note "T46" "csharp.rkt: a C# lexer that knows regular, verbatim, interpolated (nested strings, {{ }}, format specs), raw and $$ raw strings, char literals, comments, # directives, @identifiers; no dotnet needed. Bracket check with candidate-and-verify edits, ranked by indentation violations. First version blamed the outer namespace brace and put the closer at EOF when a closer was missing inside (the unmatched brace at EOF belongs to an outer block): now candidates also come from every indentation-violation site and the finding is located at the edit. Fixture deletions of every } in Shapes.cs: 21/21 detected, 21/21 verified, 14/21 restore the original (modulo the whitespace-only line a deleted brace leaves). Gate time: 2 ms on 2000 lines, 1 ms on the fixture.")
(157 "2026-09-27T04:43:45Z" "xl1" done "T46" "1 check passed")
(158 "2026-09-27T04:44:03Z" "xl1" claim "T47" "xl1")
(159 "2026-09-27T04:51:22Z" "xl1" note "T47" "steer/testparse.rkt: parse-test-output(cmd, output) detects pytest/dotnet/rackunit from the command or the output's own signature, returns failures (id file line message) in report order + a one-line summary. checks.rkt now keeps up to 1MiB of output (was a 64KiB tail only), so a long dotnet run's first failures are not lost. check-findings in cmd-tasks.rkt shows the summary in the headline plus up to 3 test-failed findings, --full/--limit lift the cap, unrecognised output falls back to the old raw tail unchanged. Fixtures are sanitized REAL runs (pytest 9.1, dotnet 10 xunit on a broken GuardClauses copy, raco test), not synthesized: tests/fixtures/test-output/. Verified end to end through steer done.")
(160 "2026-09-27T04:51:26Z" "xl1" done "T47" "1 check passed")
(161 "2026-09-27T04:51:42Z" "xl1" claim "T48" "xl1")
(162 "2026-09-27T04:56:19Z" "xl1" note "T48" "parse-diagnostics(output) in testparse.rkt: MSBuild (path(line,col): error CSnnnn: msg [proj], deduped since MSBuild prints each diagnostic twice) and Python tracebacks (last frame = where it happened, not the caller; exception chaining -> multiple failures). Only consulted in check-findings when parse-test-output found nothing, so a build failure never masquerades as a test failure; new finding kinds diag-failed/diag-failed-more and E1 class build-failed keep the distinction through to the taxonomy. Fixtures: two real dotnet builds of a broken GuardClauses copy (one syntax CS1026, one CS0103+2 warnings) and two real python3 tracebacks (ZeroDivisionError, ModuleNotFoundError).")
(163 "2026-09-27T04:56:21Z" "xl1" done "T48" "1 check passed")
(164 "2026-09-27T04:56:27Z" "xl1" claim "T49" "xl1")
(165 "2026-09-27T05:02:14Z" "xl1" note "T49" "anchors.rkt: python files resolve via python.rkt's find-anchor worker (ast, qualname = dotted path like Class.method), replacing the heuristic. Span = first decorator through end_lineno; hash = ast.dump(include_attributes=False) so formatting/comments/whitespace are invisible but a real body/decorator/rename change is not. Ambiguous bare names (e.g. 3 classes each with __init__) return not-found with the qualified candidates listed, rather than silently picking the first (the old heuristic's failure mode). Overload/singledispatch/property-setter groups resolve to the first definition and report how many later ones share the name ('shadowed). tests/unit-test.rkt's old .py heuristic example now correctly uses the python method; moved that regression case to Ruby to keep heuristic coverage.")
(166 "2026-09-27T05:02:24Z" "xl1" done "T49" "1 check passed")
(167 "2026-09-27T05:02:30Z" "xl1" claim "T50" "xl1")
(168 "2026-09-27T05:16:01Z" "xl1" note "T50" "csharp.rkt: cs-find-anchor over cs-lex's tokens; a container stack (namespace excluded from qualname; enum excluded from member-scanning, its comma-list syntax isn't the member grammar); dispatch by what follows the name: ( =method/ctor/operator/destructor, {/=> =property/indexer, ;/,/= =field(s). Overloads and same-named members across partial classes disambiguate by /arity or (type,type); a bare ambiguous name refuses with the candidate list rather than guessing. Bugs found only by testing against the real fixtures: (1) parse-params consumed the outer ')' into the last parameter's tokens, corrupting its type text and its emptiness check; (2) 'this' in the modifier list made the scanner skip straight over real indexers; (3) an auto-property's trailing '= expr;' initializer wasn't included in the span, so the initializer's own tokens (e.g. 'new Guard()') got rescanned as a phantom third constructor; (4) namespaces were never tracked, so nothing inside one was ever found. All fixed; verified against every member note 12 named (generics, attributes, decorators via #region, partial classes, file-scoped namespace, indexer, operator, destructor, event, record, generic struct) plus formatting/comment insensitivity of the hash.")
(169 "2026-09-27T05:16:13Z" "xl1" done "T50" "1 check passed")
(170 "2026-09-27T05:16:20Z" "xl1" claim "T51" "xl1")
(171 "2026-09-27T05:25:13Z" "xl1" note "T51" "Added anchor-names-in-file (racket/python.rkt/csharp.rkt) and miss-with-suggestion in anchors.rkt: resolve-anchor's not-found result now carries file-exists? and suggestions (an ambiguity list when the worker already has one, else fuzzy closest-match against every name in the file). cmd-add/cmd-import/cmd-edit --add-anchor call anchor-not-found-findings to turn this into anchor-pending (file absent: info) vs anchor-unresolved (file exists, name wrong: warning + did-you-mean), instead of both reading as the same silent 'pending'. Stored anchor records are unchanged (still just ref+hash); the diagnosis is recomputed at add/import/edit time only, not persisted.")
(172 "2026-09-27T05:25:20Z" "xl1" done "T51" "1 check passed")
(173 "2026-09-27T05:25:26Z" "xl1" claim "T52" "xl1")
(174 "2026-09-27T05:34:28Z" "xl1" note "T52" "One shared language-coverage-table in main.rkt feeds steer help syntax|stale|done (so the three texts cannot drift apart) plus a rewritten steer-code skill and README known-limits/tool-table. Two real bugs found only by testing the rendered text: Racket #<<EOF heredocs do NOT include a trailing newline (needed \\n\\n, not \\n, to get a blank line before the following paragraph), and pregexp does not support \\n or \\z as escapes (need an actual newline character, built via string-append outside the #px reader, and $ instead of \\z). Installed .claude/skills/ was stale until steer/compiled/skills_rkt.zo was deleted and steer rebuilt: skills.rkt's embedded-skills macro only re-embeds skills/ content when raco make actually recompiles that module.")
(175 "2026-09-27T05:34:31Z" "xl1" done "T52" "1 check passed")
(176 "2026-09-27T05:34:37Z" "xl1" claim "T53" "xl1")
(177 "2026-09-27T05:39:37Z" "xl1" note "T53" "Milestone gate tests/xlang-integration-test.rkt: a full agent session on a Python file and a C# file through the real CLI (no dotnet SDK needed anywhere: the one dotnet-test-shaped check output is simulated from a real sanitized fixture, proving the parser, not the SDK). Covers: init, import with an exact Class.method anchor, a body edit making it stale, a comment-only edit NOT making it stale, broken syntax -> hook exit 2 with a located verified repair, --fix, a real logic bug caught by done with file:line (pytest half skips loudly if pytest is not importable), and the anchor-pending vs anchor-unresolved distinction for both languages. 858 tests pass across tests/; make test-bin passes (58). syntax-check.rkt's only diff on this branch is an export addition (edit-text), no Racket-path logic change, so scripts/syntax-accuracy.rkt was not rerun (samples/ is not fetched in this worktree and the diff cannot have moved the numbers).")
(178 "2026-09-27T05:39:41Z" "xl1" done "T53" "1 check passed")
